// Swashbuckler: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== swashbuckler.f01 | feature | three-act
title: Red Sails In The Channel
genre: swashbuckler + revenge
tone: Salt-lashed, grand and unforgiving
era: 1343 | period
setting: The coast of Brittany and the sea lanes of the Channel | real | sea
logline: When a king beheads her husband on a charge she knows is false, a Breton noblewoman sells her castles, buys three ships, paints them black with red sails, and hunts the king's shipping for thirteen years.
premise: {jeanne} is a lady of forty-three with five children and an orderly life. Her husband is invited to a tournament under truce, arrested, and executed without public evidence; his head is sent to hang on a city gate. She takes her sons to look at it. Then she goes home and makes an inventory of everything she owns, in terms of what it will fetch.
synopsis: The first act turns a chatelaine into a captain. She sells land, plate and her own jewels. She raises four hundred men who loved her husband, takes a royal garrison by walking in as its lady and letting her men through the door, and learns that a castle can be besieged and a ship cannot. She buys three vessels. The black paint and the red sails are her own idea: she wants the king to be told who it was. The second act is the cruise, and the film lets it be thrilling: a noblewoman who has run a household of hundreds finds she can run a fleet. She fights at the boarding with an axe. She has a rule: from each prize, the nobles are killed, and two or three sailors are spared and set ashore, so that the story will reach the king. Her sailing master {pieter}, a Fleming, teaches her the Channel; her son {olivier} grows from a boy of seven toward manhood on a deck and begins to wonder what they are for. The enemy's admiral {the_admiral} hunts her without luck and with mounting respect. The cost comes due at sea: her flagship is sunk; she is five days in an open boat with two sons, and the younger dies of cold in her arms. In the third act vengeance has lost its taste and she does not know how to stop. {olivier} asks whether his father would recognise her. She lands in England, where the king's enemies make her welcome, and an English knight, {walter}, a patient and humorous man, asks nothing of her. She marries him. She goes home at last to a castle on the Breton shore, and gardens. The film's last scene is {olivier} grown, a famous soldier with a savage reputation of his own, riding in under his father's banner, and his mother looking at him as at an account she is not sure she closed.
question: When does justice turn into the thing it set out to punish?
themes: grief as an organising force; a woman's competence let off its leash; what children inherit
audience: Adults and older teenagers; lovers of sea adventure with a shadow in it
viewpoint: With {jeanne}; increasingly through {olivier}'s eyes
stakes: Her life, her children's, and what kind of people thirteen years of vengeance will make them
conflict: A widow's private war against a king, and against her own inability to end it
ending: She loses a son to the sea, lays the war down, marries, and lives quietly. Her eldest carries it on.
experience: Wind and spray and the fierce joy of the chase; then a cold open boat; then an uneasy peace
look: Black hulls and blood-red sails on a grey sea; a head on a gate; a walled garden at the end
sound: Canvas cracking, a boarding horn, axe on timber. A lullaby in Breton, once.
m: avenge | institution | justice-vs-mercy | pyrrhic
@ jeanne | Jeanne de Belleville | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 43 | The king to know her name with dread | He killed her husband by treachery | Vengeance is costing her the children it was for | Orderly, ferocious, lucid, unable to do anything by halves | From chatelaine to corsair to a woman in a garden | Courtly French; orders given like household instructions | Mail under a surcoat, a boarding axe, a widow's ring
@ olivier | Olivier | co-lead | foil | human | he/him | 7 | To be worthy of both parents | He saw the head on the gate | He is not sure this honours it | Watchful, brave, troubled | From boy to the heir of the war | Few words; asks the question no one else can | A dead man's sword too long for him
@ pieter | Pieter | supporting | mentor | human | he/him | 50 | A good ship well sailed, and his share | She pays and she listens | She will not turn for home | Weathered, practical, fond of her | From hired pilot to the one who rows the open boat | Flemish bluntness | Tarred coat, a lead line
@ the_admiral | The Admiral | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 48 | To bring her in | The king asks about her every month | He thinks the king was wrong | Honourable, tired | From hunter to a man who lets a sail go by | Formal, rueful | Royal livery, salt-stained
@ walter | Sir Walter | supporting | love-interest | human | he/him | 45 | Her company, on any terms | He has seen enough war to know what it does | She does not know how to stop | Easy, humorous, patient | From host to husband | Slow English French, teasing | A plain gown, a hawk on his wrist
~ jeanne - olivier | mother and son | she shows him the gate | he asks if his father would know her
~ jeanne - pieter | captain and sailing master | she is the paying passenger | he rows; she holds the child
~ jeanne - the_admiral | corsair and pursuer | a pirate to be hanged | an enemy he declines to find
~ jeanne - walter | exile and host | suspicion of kindness | a garden
# The Gate | EXT | City Gate | DAY | jeanne, olivier | Look | A head on a spike. She holds her sons' chins up to it. She says: remember. Then she goes home and counts the silver | {jeanne} vows vengeance | 5 | crowd=100, vfx=severed head
# The Gate | INT | Royal Garrison, Hall | NIGHT | jeanne, olivier | Take a castle | She arrives as a visiting lady. The gate is opened for her. Her men come through it | | 6 | crowd=80, stunts=castle taken
# The Cruise | EXT | Shipyard Beach | DAY | jeanne, pieter | Paint them black | Three ships. Tar, and sails dyed red in vats. {pieter} says it will be seen for miles. She says: yes | The fleet sails | 5 | water, crowd=100, vehicles=three cogs
# The Cruise | EXT | Channel, Boarding | DAY | jeanne, olivier, pieter | Take a prize | Red sails out of a squall. Grapnels. She is first across with an axe. Two sailors are put ashore alive to tell it | | 8 | water, crowd=120, stunts=boarding action, vehicles=cogs, vfx=sea battle
# The Cruise | EXT | Royal Flagship, Deck | DAY | the_admiral | Hunt her | Another burnt hull, another pair of survivors with a message. He looks at the horizon and says the king should have held a trial | | 4 | water, vehicles=cog
# The Boat | EXT | Open Boat, Channel | NIGHT | jeanne, olivier, pieter | Lose him | The flagship gone. Five days. {pieter} rows. The smaller boy stops shivering. She sings to him in Breton anyway | Her younger son dies | 7 | water, vfx=open sea at night, props=open boat
# The Boat | EXT | English Shore | DAWN | jeanne, olivier | Be asked | They carry him up the shingle. {olivier} asks whether his father would know her now. She has no answer | She lays the war down | 5 | water
# The Garden | EXT | Castle Garden, Breton Coast | DAY | jeanne, walter, olivier | Close the account | Years on. She prunes. {walter} reads. Her son rides in, a soldier with a name men fear. She looks at him a long time | | 5 | animals=horses+hawk
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the Breton noblewoman who turned corsair in 1343, under her recorded name, with invented companions
